</table>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Benchmark Task Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When viewing a tennis rally, we observe a rapid, high-frequency exchange of tactics and technique. Each rally is initiated by one player, who accelerates into position, selects a stroke type, chooses a shot direction, and ultimately produces a specific landing point or outcome. After the ball crosses the net or rebounds, the opponent proceeds through the same process. The event sequence alternates between players until a stroke ends the point. Based on this understanding of the sport, TennisTV defines a stroke event as the atomic unit, spanning movement initiation, shot selection, directional execution, and ball landing. Each rally video is modeled as a time-ordered rally sequence composed of these consecutive stroke events.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stroke Event.</span>
The stroke-level perspective targets fine-grained phenomena, where the model must recognize and localize specific stroke events such as Action Recognition (AR), Movement Grounding (MG), Hit Direction (HD) and Hit Outcome (HO). These tasks compel the model to detect subtle spatiotemporal cues at the frame level during rapid, interactive exchanges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rally Sequence.</span> The rally-level perspective centers on the macro dynamics of a point and encompasses Technique Identification (TI), Rally Count (RC), Technique Preference (TP) and Point Winner determination (PW). These tasks require a model to analyze the entire game process and perceive each stroke event, so as to grasp the overall trend of the rally and the players’ technical tendencies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, TennisTV offers a comprehensive and challenge benchmark that probes both basic perception (e.g., AR, HD) and higher-level tactical reasoning (e.g., PW, TP), providing a robust, diversified testbed for video understanding in fast-paced, high-frequency sports.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Dataset Annotation Pipeline</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TennisTV is derived from F3Set<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, which provides manual annotations of individual shots from tennis tournament videos. Based on these annotations, we perform a three-stage data cleaning and annotation pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basic Data Preparation</span>
We first aggregated the shot annotations corresponding to the same rally in temporal order and generated basic rally-level technical statistics, such as rally length and the distribution of different strokes used by both players, to construct structured rally information. Next, we removed videos with an insufficient number of rallies from the original dataset. Moreover, because tennis tournaments are played on courts with diverse surfaces, the varying background colors can reduce viewing clarity. To address this issue, we employed Qwen2.5VL-7B<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> as a video quality judge model to filter out videos with poor visual quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion Information Extraction</span>
Secondly, the original annotations primarily capture the ball’s trajectory within individual stroke events, while overlooking the player’s movement direction and positional changes throughout the rally. To address this limitation, we developed a player motion extraction method to capture movement information between stroke events. For each video, we extracted <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> originally annotated stroke frames from the structured rally information obtained in the first stage. We then paired adjacent frames to form <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1</annotation></semantics></math> stroke frame pairs. For each pair, we applied a lightweight object detector to localize the player’s position. By computing the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta y</annotation></semantics></math> offsets between the bounding box coordinates across the two frames, we determined movement direction: if either offset exceeded a specific threshold <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the player is considered to have moved in that direction. Finally, the extracted player motion information was integrated into the structured rally information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question Generation</span>
We employed GPT-4o to aggregate the structured rally information and generated a comprehensive rally sequence description for each video. Using the rally descriptions and basic statistics, we generated 8 task questions using either GPT-4o or a predefined template. All questions were manually verified, resulting in a total of 2500 questions across 1298 videos.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate 13 open-source MLLMs that can process multi-frame video inputs, including 8 non-thinking models: Video-LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> , LLaVA-OneVision-7B, Qwen2.5VL-3B&amp;7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> , Qwen2VL-7B, mPLUG-Owl3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> , InternVideo2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and MiMoVL-SFT-7B-2508 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> , as well as 5 thinking models: Video-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> , VideoChat-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> , VideoRFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> , VersaVid-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and MiMoVL-RL-7B-2508 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> . In addition, we assess GPT-4o, GPT-4.1 and Gemini2.5-Pro through their APIs. For the open-source models, we followed the input frame settings in VideoMME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> . For the closed-source models, we adopted a video sampling rate of 1 FPS for evaluation due to budget consideration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Evaluation Metric</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt two different metrics to evaluate the model performance on TennisTV. For PW task, which aims to identify which player won the rally, the model is provided with a statement describing the outcome of the rally. The model’s answer is considered correct only if it correctly classifies the statement as True or False. For other tasks, we formulate them as multiple-choice questions and evaluate whether the model’s selected option is correct.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results</span>
Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 TennisTV Benchmark ‣ TennisTV: Do Multimodal Large Language Models Understand Tennis Rallies?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports results on TennisTV. Among closed-source models, GPT-4.1 ranks first overall at 45.0%, edging GPT-4o and Gemini 2.5 Pro. Open-source systems are competitive: the strongest non-thinking baseline, Qwen2.5-VL-7B, reaches 39.4%, and the thinking variant VersaVid-R1 nudges this to 39.8%. Reasoning brings targeted gains on the same 7B backbone (RC improves from 22.3% to 27.0% and TI from 45.6% to 47.8%) while averages remain close. Leadership is task-specific: Gemini 2.5 Pro leads MG (41.7%), GPT-4.1 leads TP (52.0%), and GPT-4o attains the highest AvgRS (48.1%). Despite these gains, overall accuracies remain modest and only moderately above the random baseline, indicating substantial headroom for progress.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15602v1/task_correlation.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:405/331;" width="405" height="331" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Correlation matrix between 8 tasks in TennisTV.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding I — High degree of correlation among the AR, TI, and TP tasks.</span>
As shown in Fig <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ TennisTV: Do Multimodal Large Language Models Understand Tennis Rallies?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we observe a strong positive association among AR, TI, and TP. Performance improvements on any one of these tasks are consistently accompanied by gains on the other two, and this pattern holds across model families and frame-sampling protocols. Conceptually, the three tasks constitute a coherent capability cluster: identifying a specific action provides the primitives for technique identification, which in turn supports inferring tactic preferences. Practically, this correlation implies that progress on one task is predictive of progress on the others, and that ablations targeting low-level attribution or temporal stitching can transfer to tactic inference. Taken together, this coherence offers empirical support for the soundness of our benchmark task design.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Ablation study of different frame sampling strategies on Qwen2.5VL-7B. We report the AvgSE, AvgRS and Avg metrics.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frame Sampling</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AvgSE</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AvgRS</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;">1 FPS</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;">37.5</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;">39.2</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;">38.3</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;">16 Frames</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;">38.1</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">39.8</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">39.0</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;">32 Frames</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">39.3</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">39.5</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">39.4</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;">64 Frames</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">38.7</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:6.5pt;padding-right:6.5pt;">38.5</td>
