</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding II — Optimal frame sampling density differs by task.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ TennisTV: Do Multimodal Large Language Models Understand Tennis Rallies?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, with Qwen2.5-VL-7B, overall performance peaks at 32 frames and further increasing the number of input frames yields diminishing or even negative returns, with the rally sequence metrics particularly affected. These patterns indicate that finer temporal resolution primarily benefits brief, high-frequency stroke cues, while rally-level reasoning saturates earlier and is more sensitive to redundancy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15602v1/none_knowledge_1.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/202;" width="476" height="202"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Visualization of performance on three TennisTV benchmark tasks, which require no prior knowledge of tennis, based on the same base model.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding III — Reasoning brings gains on knowledge-light tasks.</span>
To disentangle the effect of reasoning from tennis-specific priors, we analyze three knowledge-light tasks (MG, HD, RC) that are deliberately designed to minimize dependence on specialized domain knowledge (e.g., scoring rules). As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ TennisTV: Do Multimodal Large Language Models Understand Tennis Rallies?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, three reasoning variants (VideoChat-R1, VideoRFT and VersaVid-R1) are derived from reinforcement learning post-training applied to the same Qwen2.5-VL-7B. With the backbone, tokenization scheme, and video interface held constant, the observed improvements over the non-reasoning baseline can be attributed to enhanced reasoning rather than architectural differences or domain-specific priors. In addition, we employ the chain-of-thought prompting method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> to evaluate the original reasoning capability of the base model. Relative gains are most pronounced on RC and HD, where multi-step analysis and explicit temporal/trajectory reasoning are critical, and are steadier but smaller on MG. This pattern supports the view that explicit reasoning chiefly aids information aggregation, while offering more limited benefits on purely temporal-oriented tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Takeaways</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on our findings, we highlight two takeaways
for future improvements in tennis video understanding:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Balancing frame sampling density across tasks.</span>
For tennis video understanding, the sampling frequency that yields the best performance differs between coarse global tasks and fine-grained tasks. Future work should pursue a principled balance between event granularity and sampling density, so that models capture long range context without losing the detailed temporal cues required for rapid event recognition.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Enhancing the temporal understanding ability of video reasoning models.</span>
The experimental results have proved that reasoning can improve rally-level understanding, but it has not benefited from fine-grained temporal grounding task. This reveals that current video reasoning methods still lack sufficient capabilities for temporal perception.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce TennisTV, the first benchmark for tennis video understanding specifically designed to evaluate the capabilities of MLLMs. TennisTV models each rally as a time-ordered sequence of consecutive stroke events, using automated pipelines for filtering and question generation. Unified evaluations over diverse MLLMs show that reasoning and careful frame sampling help, yet models still struggle with the sport’s fast, information-dense dynamics. We expect TennisTV to spur advances in hierarchical temporal reasoning and robust sports video understanding.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
